UPDATE Beats SET [Text]=N'Sigrun called Teo down to the co-op office before she said anything to anyone else, which was itself a kind of announcement, because Sigrun didn''t usually rehearse a thing in front of one person before she said it in front of forty. The office was a converted household two decks below the commons, small enough that the wall display took up most of one side of it, and the number on it — three hundred sixty-eight meters — sat behind Sigrun''s shoulder the whole time she talked, so that Teo found herself reading it past the woman''s head without meaning to.

"I''m going to abstain," Sigrun said, no preamble, the same directness she used on the households. "When the vote comes. I wanted you to hear it before I said it to anyone else, because you''re the only person in this bloc who''ll understand exactly what that does to the arithmetic, and I''d rather you hear it from me than work it out from a tally afterward and wonder why I didn''t tell you first."

Teo didn''t answer immediately. She was doing, despite herself, exactly the arithmetic Sigrun had predicted — one fewer voice on either side, one more in the column that changed nothing about which way the room tipped and everything about whether the chair who''d run every meeting had a position at all.

"You''ve held this whole thing together by not having a side," Teo said finally. "I don''t think that''s news to you."

"It''s not," Sigrun said. "I''ve had a side for a while, if I''m honest with myself about it, and I''m not going to tell you which one, because that''s exactly the thing I''ve decided not to do. I''ve watched Priya''s number and Wen''s shop sit across from each other for weeks now, and I don''t think this bloc needs its chair adding a third weight to either side of that scale. I think it needs someone who ran the meetings fairly enough that nobody can point at the outcome afterward and say the process bent it. Abstaining is the only vote that doesn''t cost me that."

She sat down, finally, on the edge of the desk instead of behind it, which Teo had learned over the year meant Sigrun was about to say something she''d already decided was true and wasn''t fully comfortable having decided.

"Is that cowardice?" she asked. "I want the honest answer, not the kind you''d give the room."

Teo thought about it the way she thought about a load she hadn''t finished trimming, turning it over before she committed a figure to the ledger. "I don''t think so," she said. "But I don''t think my saying that settles it for you, because you didn''t ask me because you trust my judgment on cowardice. You asked because you wanted to say the word out loud to someone before you had to hear other people say it about you, and I think you already know what you believe. You just wanted company on the way to believing it in public."

Sigrun almost smiled at that, the closest Teo had seen her come to it in weeks. "That''s a fair read," she admitted. "Say the other part anyway. The part where you actually answer it."

"Abstention is a vote," Teo said. "It''s on the tally same as yes and no. It just doesn''t sound like one, because it doesn''t move anything by itself, and people confuse not moving something with not having chosen. You''ve chosen. You''ve chosen not to be the reason it goes one way instead of the other, and I don''t think that''s the same as having no opinion. I think it''s you deciding that your opinion isn''t the kind that belongs in this particular column, and holding to that even though it costs you something to hold to it in front of forty people who''ll wonder."

"They''ll wonder which way I actually lean," Sigrun said. "Some of them will decide I''m hiding it out of weakness. A few will decide I''ve already made a private deal with one side or the other and abstention''s just the cover for it. There isn''t a version of this where everyone reads it the way I mean it."

"No," Teo agreed. "There isn''t. I don''t think that''s a reason not to do it, though. I think you''re the only person in this bloc whose job depends on the room believing the process was fair more than it depends on anyone believing you personally agreed with the outcome. Sell that too hard by having an opinion and you lose the thing that actually makes you useful to all forty-one households instead of twenty-three of them or seventeen."

Sigrun looked at the wall display a while, at the number that would be repainted lower again on Monday whether she''d decided anything today or not. "I keep thinking," she said, "that somebody in my position ought to have a clean answer for whether abstaining is honest or just tidy. I don''t have one. I have a decision I can live with and a word — cowardice — that I can''t quite set down even after making it."

"Then don''t set it down," Teo said. "Carry it into the room with you instead of pretending it isn''t there. I think that''s more honest than arriving certain."

"That''s not comforting," Sigrun said.

"No," Teo agreed again. "I don''t have a comforting version of it. I''ve got a true one, and you asked for that instead."

Sigrun nodded, once, the way she nodded to close a meeting, and stood up from the desk edge, and the number on the wall behind her stayed exactly where it had been the whole conversation, patient, unbothered by either of them, the one thing in the room that had never once needed to decide what it believed about itself.

She said it to the households two nights later, in the commons, in the same flat voice she used for descent schedules and thirty-day windows, nothing dressed up and nothing apologized for. "When the vote comes, I''ll be abstaining. I''m telling you now instead of on the night itself, because I don''t want anyone reading a silent ballot as a surprise. I''m not undecided the way you might be undecided. I''ve decided this is the vote I owe the process, not the outcome." Nobody asked her to explain further, and she didn''t offer to. Teo, watching from the wall she''d claimed at every meeting since the first one, thought that the room had taken the news the way it took most things lately — without argument, filed next to Priya''s shortfall and Wen''s jigs as one more true thing nobody had the standing to talk anyone out of.' WHERE Id='019F3AC8-397D-7627-87C2-350828A85D7E';